I agree with you that faith is  definitely essential to establish human  rights on an individual level because  that is what is going to motivate you  but I think that one of the primary  problems that we're seeing in the world  today is that faith is not a generic  term faith is very specific and so when  you use the word faith it means  different things to different people  when you if you're coming for example  from Christian tradition generally faith  is not attached to a specific legal code  whereas when you're talking to a Muslim  then there would be attached in a very  complex legal code and so it's easier  for a person coming from a Christian  tradition to agree to a generic even for  to a certain degree secular standard of  human rights because from his tradition  there is no specific religious standard  is very generic I'm being relatively  obviously whereas for coming from a  Muslim tradition and probably in from a  Jewish tradition you would have a far  more legalistic far more specific value  system and so I think the clash would  come not faith faithful versus faithless  but rather from within the faith  traditions themselves so it's just like  a comment to me a good it's a good  comment and i would say briefly in  response concurring in your comment but  human rights formulations in many ways  provide mirrors in which religious  communities can reflect upon their own  normative traditions their own canonical  formulations of what the good life in  the good society happened to be and to  articulate in their own terms what views  what values what practices what  institutional structures are consonant  with sometimes go beyond the human  rights values articulated in these  universal declarations and many times  what happens is when give the 48  declaration or the 66 covenant or other  formulations like that are used as a way  of thinking about how do we in our  millennium long tradition think about  these goods there's a lot of overlap my  colleague of Diwaniyah Nayeem is a  scholar who's been working out of the  Sufi tradition he's a victim of  ostracism without trial from his own  native Sudan he knows what it means to  be a victim of human rights abuses but  what he has spent his life  what I'm doing is to look at the Quran  and the hadith and some of the schools  of jurisprudence before the closing of  the door and and to think hard through  what those particular canonical texts  and initial jurisprudential reflections  offer to the 20th and 21st century for  Muslims and non-muslims alike about  human rights now sometimes the terms  don't match many times the practices  don't match but nonetheless what he  argues is that there is an awful lot of  overlap between some of the great  teachings of the Prophet some of the  injunctions of hadith and some of what  we now take to be human rights claims  Muslim communities are doing that in  different parts of the non-arabic muslim  world jewish communities are doing that  looking back at the hebrew bible and  Talmudic exegesis of the same Hindu  scholars Buddhist scholars Confucian  scholars Taoist scholars alike are doing  that and inevitably inevitably you need  a big rosetta stone with seven or eight  what the lines of text running at the  same time to keep the languages straight  inevitably you have to recognize they're  going to be sharp differences I propose  the question of Rachel that there may be  sharp differences in which there is no  overlapping consensus but it's  remarkable how many basic values the  life the property to maintenance of  one's name to the ability to get married  to the care of one's child to the basic  security one's home that these religious  communities paint for granted as part of  the original canonical formulations even  if they embroider them in a variety of  different ways in that sense I think you  know human rights today are very useful  ingredients for thinking about common  values together knowing that they're not  the only formulation they're by no means  the superior formulation but there  nonetheless a useful grid for thinking  about what we hold together and how we  can formulate relationships together  that are politically and culturally and  socially cogent
 

